A fully folded struct instance stored in a constant may become a const record.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Defaults = |
        title String,
        year Int,
    |

    defaults #= Defaults("Moth", 2026)
    site_title #= defaults.title
]

### Contract

A const record is a data-only compile-time member group. Every supplied value
must fold. Field projection may participate in another constant expression.

The complete record is unavailable as an ordinary runtime value. It cannot be
assigned to a runtime binding, passed as a runtime argument, returned from a
runtime function, stored in a collection or map or used through a runtime
receiver method.

Use a folded field when runtime code needs its ordinary value:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    io.line(defaults.title)
]

A projection whose result is another const record retains the same restriction.
Project further to an ordinary value before runtime use.

Struct-backed const records preserve their named struct's fields and types.
Anonymous const records retain named folded fields without declaring a
constructable type or introducing structural runtime typing. Their existing
folded export and provenance rules remain in force.

### Anonymous const records

Anonymous construction uses the named-only form of
@../language-overview/#mon-syntax (MON syntax):

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    labels #= (
        first = "Priya",
        second = "Rob",
    )

    single #= (name = "Moth")
    empty #= ()
]

Every field has a unique name and an explicit initializer. Types are inferred
from those values. Labels create fields, not local declarations or parameters.
`#=` imposes the compile-time requirement. Ordinary `=` does not become a const
record merely because its fields could fold.

### Nested values

A field may contain an inline anonymous const record, an already declared const
record or an explicitly constructed nominal value that satisfies constant rules.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Size = |
        width Int,
        height Int,
    |

    settings #= (
        window = (
            size = Size(width = 8, height = 4),
            title = "Strategy",
        ),
    )

    width #= settings.window.size.width
]

All nested initializers must fold. Initializers use the surrounding scope,
not names introduced by earlier fields. Nesting introduces neither arbitrary
compile-time execution nor implicit construction of a named type.

Input contracts belong to declarations rather than record entries. Exact
configuration placement and processing rules are owned by
@../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build configuration values).
The MON format's serialisation domain is an accepted Rust-only contract owned by @../mon/#mon-format (MON format); its codec is queued, and it does not change const-record evaluation or source syntax.
